  "tour": {
    "title": "Wrath, Substitution, and Victory at the Cross",
    "intro": "This tour starts by mapping the main ways Christians have explained what Jesus’ death accomplished, then lets you hear a clear propitiatory and penal-substitution account. From there it turns to BibleProject’s substantially different reading of Jesus’ “cup,” hears N. T. Wright argue that victory and substitution belong together, and ends with an explicit claim that Jesus took wrath on himself. The point is not to force agreement, but to let the disagreements become audible.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "radically-christian:podcast:e9106bdd-https-radicallychristian-com-p-9457",
        "startSec": 288,
        "endSec": 440,
        "why": "Begin with a map of the debate. The teachers say that atonement theories ask “how does the death of one man cover or take away the guilt of the entire world?” and introduce Christus Victor as Jesus “waging war” against the forces of darkness.",
        "episodeTitle": "Theories of Atonement – CrossTalk S4E10",
        "sourceId": "radically-christian",
        "source": "Radically Christian",
        "audioUrl": "https://substackcdn.com/video_upload/post/148682238/4202feae-310f-48d3-8260-2bd297e7ec54/transcoded.mp3?post_id=148682238&relation=podcast&Expires=1785437879&Key-Pair-Id=APKAIVDA3NPSMPSPESQQ&Signature=E81CSoqx5J2xZCnTPRkPrWvRg17tmjM-2SKhycuZSAMk4XOl732-KwOVFWlcApEglSliBQA4R8rvfsVLBkCl5wLrSJeFJE8YFypZehk1S-ztVhFWUOaXbBbN9Ljnj9yk4CxD8CvqZvUeUpm0st083KKQu8uajFMmkOZhz7FeZXgC6Xmc9frzupTiWp9gV1U1qj14AnTiRqjJoBIVkwTkQzHh0KCRmLXCs0pKbEAEz-H7qAjGYKr0-w04CQCM2GTYNZ2q8WWm8SHYOfihsTvcGwCbh48uSLygBXDGvyuPHDZzeDCDWp8K5zjgnJbXnWNCyt8aDP-dX-e4oZzV77TM1Q__",
        "durationSec": 2917,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:bacb1fc2-com-e79bf8f7-6ab5-30f3-9e4f-46bbb41dd164",
        "startSec": 1180,
        "endSec": 1540,
        "why": "This is the clearest traditional propitiatory and penal account in the tour. The teacher connects Jesus to the Day of Atonement and says that “the wrath of God that sin deserves” is, in some sense, “poured out on Jesus,” so that “the penalty for sin has been fully and finally dealt with.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans 3:21-26",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/zhvgac/romans_321-368t8hu.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1954,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:two-men-named-jesus",
        "startSec": 1510,
        "endSec": 2000,
        "why": "Now hear a direct alternative to that formulation. BibleProject reads Jesus’ cup through Israel’s judgment and Barabbas: Jesus “will poke the bear and allow Rome to kill him instead of the whole people,” then explicitly says this is “totally different” from modern Protestant conceptions of Jesus bearing God’s wrath on the cross.",
        "episodeTitle": "Two Men Named Jesus",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/2d2b5ff7-d5a7-4f12-b496-d2e799ece9e0/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=2d2b5ff7-d5a7-4f12-b496-d2e799ece9e0",
        "durationSec": 3582,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:32b053a0-4d5c-11ee-8683-f1dd20c5e5ec",
        "startSec": 1380,
        "endSec": 1600,
        "why": "Wright supplies a mediating synthesis rather than simply choosing one theory. He says the cross wins “a victory over the dark force” through Jesus taking the sinner’s place, and concludes that it is “definitely substitutionary” and “definitely penal,” but within the larger story of God dealing with the powers.",
        "episodeTitle": "#185 Questions on Crucifixion and Atonement (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR9770585049.mp3?updated=1749653873",
        "durationSec": 2261,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:d50324b1-cc4e-4295-a992-55319e5ada78",
        "startSec": 3760,
        "endSec": 3940,
        "why": "End with the strongest contrasting affirmation of wrath being borne by Jesus. The speaker says Jesus comes “as a master of wrath” and, “by his taking on the wrath for all guilt,” executes justice against himself; the same clip also says the cross judged and defeated the spiritual powers.",
        "episodeTitle": "Nahum Introduction: God Will Not Clear the Guilty",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG6248171917.mp3?updated=1664486074",
        "durationSec": 3977,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You have now heard three distinct emphases: a classic account in which guilt, penalty, and divine wrath are dealt with in Jesus; a BibleProject reading in which the cup chiefly means Jesus entering Israel’s destructive fate under Rome rather than God punishing Jesus; and Wright’s attempt to hold penal substitution and Christus Victor together as “victory through substitution.” The teachers therefore do not all answer your opening question in the same way—and that difference is part of what this tour is meant to clarify.",
    "totalSeconds": 1402
  },
